negative-p and NaN-perplexity events in the 250 transition-region trajectories while preserving exactly the same per-point official convergence fractions as smaller-dt Euler (0.90,0.96,0.92,0.92,0.92). Public Euler produced negative p in 150/250 and NaN perplexity in 144/250, localized to indices 17..19.","Against smaller-dt Euler, exp-public true-endpoint soft-Hamming error remained extremely close: paired MAE 2.25e-4 to 3.12e-4 across indices 17..21, with 100% convergence-status agreement. This supports the interpretation that the low-above-0.01 defect is a discretization artifact, not a required property of the continuous replicator dynamics.","The public filtered last-above-threshold error remained much more integration-step sensitive: paired MAE 0.0129 to 0.0171 between exp-public and scaled Euler despite near-identical true endpoints. Thus Figure-1-style filtered observables should not be treated as integrator-invariant endpoints.","In a sparse 10-repetition check from tau_p=0.0215 through 1000, public Euler and exp-public had identical convergence fractions at every tested point and near-identical true endpoint medians in the slow/high-tau regime; method differences were concentrated in near-threshold reported values rather than gross regime topology. This sparse check is insufficient to claim full-curve equivalence."],"scope_guard":["The result concerns numerical integration and observable collection for the tested public equations/settings; it does not refute the paper's continuous-time Lyapunov/fixed-point analysis.","The sparse high-tau check uses only 10 repetitions and cannot replace the planned full 100-point matched 50-repetition sensitivity sweep.","Multiplicative/exponential stepping is a first-order positivity-preserving sensitivity control, not asserted to be the uniquely correct continuous-time solver."],"frontier_nonempty":true,"exact_next_action":"Complete the matched full 100-point tau_p sensitivity map, using the exponential/simplex-preserving p update and the public seed policy, with 50 repetitions where tractable and an explicitly validated compiled float32 implementation for stiff low-tau points; compare true endpoint, convergence, public filtered endpoint and convergence-time surrogates against the existing public-Euler audit. Extend the continuous scaled-dt Euler rule through the transition region to map the dt=0.01 branch discontinuity. Then resume matched instantaneous-softmax versus finite-tau_p sweeps over T and tau_x to search for a regime where slow routing itself has a statistically robust benefit."}